==English==

===Etymology 1===
Origin unknown.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|fadg}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To be [[suitable]] ({{m|en|with}} or {{m|en|to}} something).
#* Wycherley
#*: Well, Sir, how '''fadges''' the new design?
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[agree]], to get along ({{m|en|with}}).
#* Milton
#*: They shall be made, spite of antipathy, to '''fadge''' together.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To get on well; to [[cope]], to [[thrive]].
#* {{RQ:Flr Mntgn Essays}}, II.17:
#*: I can never '''fadge''' well: for I am at such a stay, that except for health and life, there is nothing I will take the paines to fret my selfe about, or will purchase at so high a rate as to trouble my wits for it, or be constrained thereunto.
# {{lb|en|Geordie}} To [[eat]] together.
# {{lb|en|Yorkshire|of a horse}} To move with a [[gait]] between a [[jog]] and a [[trot]].

===Etymology 2===
Etymology uncertain.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Ireland}} Irish [[potato bread]]; a flat [[farl]], griddle-baked, often served fried.
# {{lb|en|New Zealand}} A wool pack, traditionally made of [[jute]], now often synthetic.
# {{lb|en|Geordie}} A small loaf or bun made with left-over dough.
# {{lb|en|Yorkshire}} A [[gait]] of horses between a [[jog]] and a [[trot]].

